WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.085
- I've been hearing this sound everywhere.

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:02.930
(electronic hi-hat)

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.910
That blazing fast hi-hat burst.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.020
Have you heard it?

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.053
It's all over.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.351
So what is it and how
did it end up everywhere.

00:00:19.351 --> 00:00:23.602
(electronic feedback and hi-hat)

00:00:23.602 --> 00:00:25.760
- So these crazy fast hi-hat triplets,

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.600
they are the hallmark of the trap sound,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.990
a sound that's been
steadily working its way

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.330
from the Atlanta streets
into the mainstream

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.719
for nearly two decades.

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:34.890
Nahre and I are gonna try
to make a beat inspired

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.470
by the trap sound, but first,
where did it come from?

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.330
Today, T.I. and Gucci
Mane are both claiming

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:42.740
to be fathers of the genre,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.344
and no disrespect to the two
major innovators of trap music,

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:49.340
but trap's roots actually go
back further to the mid 90s.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.700
That's when the term trap
really started showing up

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.070
in hip hop lyrics.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.500
The word was popularized
in Atlanta as slang

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.630
for a place where drug deals go down.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.800
- It's about a lifestyle
where you are dealing drugs

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.060
and it's a trap, right, that
you can't really get out of

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.480
or you can go to jail
or something like that.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.960
- References to the trap as
a place started appearing

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.142
in music by many Atlanta
rappers, for instance,

00:01:10.142 --> 00:01:13.413
the 1995 song "Thought
Process" by Goodie Mob.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.557
- Rap music is the CNN of
the black community, right,

00:01:21.557 --> 00:01:23.772
and trap music is one that is unique

00:01:23.772 --> 00:01:27.430
in that it is speaking
to some of the cultural

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.730
and economic disparities that
exist in our society today

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.520
and so a lot of the youth can relate.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.800
- That brings us to the
origin of the trap sound,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.422
and those roots extend even
further back into the 80s

00:01:37.422 --> 00:01:39.240
with the invention of an instrument

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.800
that had a massive impact
on hip hop, dance music,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.503
and pop music, the Roland TR-808.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.350
When the 808 was released in
1980 it instantly stood out

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.505
but not in a good way.

00:01:50.505 --> 00:01:54.044
Instead of using prerecorded
samples of actual drum sounds,

00:01:54.044 --> 00:01:56.620
the 808 tried to mimic real drum

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.130
with synthesized,
electronically-produced beats.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.786
Imagine a robot describing
what a drum sounds like.

00:02:01.786 --> 00:02:04.090
- (robot voice) Oh that
sweet sweet juicy boom.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.740
- Most of the producers in the early 1980s

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.770
did not like the strange, synthy sounds.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.480
That's when a stock pile of 808s

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.498
invaded neighborhood
thrift stores, and soon,

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:13.410
young creators found a cheap way

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.290
to experiment with new sounds.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.810
Eventually the 808 became standard gear

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.940
for any hopeful hip hop producer.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.971
In the 2015 documentary on the 808,

00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:25.900
Questlove even called it
the rock guitar of hip hop.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.243
Here it is, the Roland TR-808.

00:02:28.243 --> 00:02:30.510
- The boom that you hear
when you're in a car

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.610
and you hear that bass be like boom,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.700
that's typically the 808 boom
and the 808 authentic source

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.540
is from this actual machine,
which is analog technology.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.440
The sounds in here,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.400
every trap producer today constantly uses.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.490
This is how you dial up
every single individual sound

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.940
that you're gonna use.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.330
- I wanna go back to the cowbell.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.324
- Yeah, go back to your
cowbell, CB, tap it in.

00:02:51.324 --> 00:02:52.880
(synthesizer sounds)

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.510
What makes this special is
the iconic, legendary status

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.430
of the instrument itself.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.500
Now the technology has
advanced to the point

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.150
where an 808 is irrelevant,

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.520
because you can use any software program

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.870
and get absolute amazing dynamics.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:07.780
- Yeah, just on steroids.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.060
- Completely not needed
at all. (LA laughs)

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.544
- This is the essential seasoning,

00:03:12.544 --> 00:03:15.430
but you're not using it
in just any ole kitchen.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.350
- Absolutely.
- I dig it.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.170
The 808 was crucial to
creating crunk in Memphis

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.150
and bounce in New Orleans,
forerunners to the trap sound.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:24.750
You can hear the electronic hi-hats

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:29.750
on Juvenile's 1998 album, 400 Degreez.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.610
Atlanta in the early 2000 is when trap

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:41.760
really comes together.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.260
Soon the trap trinity emerged:

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.750
T.I., Young Jeezy, and Gucci Mane.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:47.970
But behind every big name,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.940
there's usually a producer
playing a key role

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.520
in shaping their sound.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.641
- We made all the stuff they
was rapping on. (laughs)

00:03:54.641 --> 00:03:58.976
So without them, without
Toomp, without Shawty Redd,

00:03:58.976 --> 00:04:02.250
you wouldn't know who T.I. is or Gucci.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:03.800
- But a lot of people
don't recognize the names

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.320
of the originators of the trap sound,

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.730
like DJ Toomp and Shawty Redd.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.970
It wasn't until the next
generation of trap music

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.180
that the producers started
to get recognition.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.880
It was 2008 and 17 year
old beat maker Lex Luger

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.460
was uploading his beats to MySpace

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.010
where they caught the
attention of Waka Flocka Flame.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.047
Luger ended up producing Waka's hit single

00:04:21.047 --> 00:04:21.997
"Hard in Da Paint".

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.750
Before long, Lex Luger and
other Atlanta based producers,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.750
like Metro Boomin', were
producing hits for major artists

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:38.890
from all over, like Snoop
Dogg, Drake, Kanye West.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.900
So not a whole lot of people are familiar

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.600
with Shawty Redd or DJ Toomp,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.543
but Metro Boomin',
that's a household name.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.810
How do you think producers
of trap gained as much fame

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.800
as the rappers that were
rapping on their beats,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.060
how did that happen?

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.340
- The tags. (laughs)

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.850
The tags and the rappers
starting to shoutout

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.390
and actually put producer on.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.830
- You might recognize Metro
Boomin' producer tag in songs

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.393
like "Father Stretch My
Hands Pt. 1" by Kanye.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.890
- Back when Redd and them were doing it,

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.740
they weren't doing tags
so you didn't know who

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:18.573
was making the beats.

00:05:18.573 --> 00:05:21.167
We'd start shouting them out,

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:25.330
"Mike WiLL Made-It", Gucci
Mane slayed it. (LA Laughs)

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.048
Once the rappers started
shouting the producers out,

00:05:28.048 --> 00:05:30.500
producers became big.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.680
- Soon, everyone wanted a piece
of Atlanta's trap producers.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.010
Pop stars like Beyonce,
Rihanna, even Miley Cyrus

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.170
all came knocking and
the Atlanta sound spread

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:40.170
around the world.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.890
International producers started using it

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.513
in everything from reggaeton to K-pop.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:55.270
In 2017, rap and R&amp;B surpassed
rock as the biggest genre

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:57.657
in the US, based on
album sales and streams.

00:05:57.657 --> 00:06:01.200
And trap is arguably rap's
most popular subgenre.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.640
That means millions of Americans
of every shade, right now,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.340
are listening to music that
invokes the cultural legacy

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.143
and ongoing struggles of segregation,

00:06:09.143 --> 00:06:12.480
structural racism, and urban violence.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.734
- Think about somebody like T.I., right,

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:19.630
he talks about the
consequences of what it means

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.430
to be a trapper, and he uses the music

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.280
to kind of work through that.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.660
This is really interesting
to me how everything

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.720
is all trap everything, and
I joke with my students,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.870
it's like you have trap
yoga, trap in paint,

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.773
trap karaoke, trap water.

00:06:34.122 --> 00:06:36.740
And I am a trap purist, I
really need to get a shirt.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.130
It basically means sitting
on the porch, so to speak,

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:43.972
and shaking my fist like
this isn't trap. (laughs)

00:06:43.972 --> 00:06:48.160
But I'm slowly but surely
coming to grips with the fact

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.489
that trap music is now trap aesthetics.

00:06:51.489 --> 00:06:55.500
It does have that kind of
distinct print that can be used

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.130
to update or remix other genres
that are already in place.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.975
But I also wish that folks
would also give receipts

00:07:02.975 --> 00:07:04.990
and credit where credit is due.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:05.850
This didn't just come out of nowhere,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.390
trap didn't just come out of nowhere.

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.700
- So now that we know where
trap music comes from,

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.610
let's break down how we
come up with trap sounds.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.590
Trap music has a wide range of tempos.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.310
It ranges from 110 BPM

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.360
to about 140.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.340
I chose 126 BPM, because
it just felt good.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:24.690
When I think of trap music,

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.330
this is about the tempo that I go to.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:28.523
I started with the 808.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.400
That heavy bass note, that's
what we mean by 808 nowadays.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.830
Next I added in that
hallmark sound, the hi-hats,

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.420
and they go at these alarmingly fast rates

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:43.170
of 32nd note triplets
or 16th note triplets.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.086
(electronic hi-hat)

00:07:46.086 --> 00:07:49.680
Trying to play that on a live
gig would be a nightmare.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.920
That's part of the reason
why trap is so intoxicating,

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:54.080
that's why it's so appealing,

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.740
because it's almost like
you're hearing the impossible

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.890
being captured, you know what I'm saying.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:02.256
Next I added in the
snare. (electronic snare)

00:08:02.256 --> 00:08:05.520
The snare just sits right
in the middle of the mix.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.157
This right here is pretty
much the basic, bare bones

00:08:09.157 --> 00:08:13.829
of trap sound, anything else is extra.

00:08:13.829 --> 00:08:16.350
(electronic beat)

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.620
So Nahre created the sound
bank of a bunch of sounds

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.560
that she made, maybe like a
call and response kind of thing.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:23.977
Here it is, it's like (imitates notes).

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.730
I got all these waves
working in like a respond,

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.920
open space kind of thing,
it sounds like this.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:35.837
(electronic music)

00:08:38.761 --> 00:08:40.496
This is everything put together.

00:08:40.496 --> 00:08:42.913
(trap music)

00:08:44.825 --> 00:08:47.312
Nahre And I, we collabed
on this trap beat.

00:08:47.312 --> 00:08:48.213
- Alright.

00:08:48.213 --> 00:08:50.273
- Alright, it should pop up right there.

00:08:50.273 --> 00:08:52.106
- Ah, found it, ha ha.

00:08:53.147 --> 00:08:55.391
- Make it loud, turn
it up loud if you can.

00:08:55.391 --> 00:08:57.808
(trap music)

00:09:02.353 --> 00:09:05.325
For some advice, what would
you suggest we could do

00:09:05.325 --> 00:09:06.842
to make the beat more trappy,

00:09:06.842 --> 00:09:09.353
what could we do to improve it?

00:09:09.353 --> 00:09:13.603
- I think that sounds trap
enough to me, I like it.

00:09:15.391 --> 00:09:18.290
I like the bounce on it, for real though.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.333
- Well Marz, man, I really
appreciate you coming though.

00:09:21.333 --> 00:09:23.123
- Definitely, and keep in touch,

00:09:23.123 --> 00:09:24.290
we can collab or something, man.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.773
- [LA] Absolutely, for sure.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:29.010
- Let us know what you
think of our trap beat

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:30.390
and make your own.

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:32.390
Download the sound bank
that I created for LA

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.490
from our description and please subscribe.

00:09:35.573 --> 00:09:37.990
(trap music)

